WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.898
DeepJoint is a robust survival model for clinical data.

00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:08.199
In medicine, when and which tests get ordered is
itself informative,

00:00:08.279 --> 00:00:10.792
reflecting how clinicians treat patients,

00:00:10.872 --> 00:00:13.828
a phenomenon the authors call clinical presence.

00:00:13.908 --> 00:00:17.749
When these patterns shift, standard prediction
models degrade.

00:00:17.829 --> 00:00:24.832
DeepJoint is a multi-task recurrent network that
models three clinical presence processes jointly with survival,

00:00:24.912 --> 00:00:28.690
delivering both strong discrimination and greater
robustness.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:35.279
Observational medical data arise from the
interaction between patients and the healthcare system.

00:00:35.359 --> 00:00:37.289
When a clinician orders a test,

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:41.503
its timing and its existence carry information
about the patient.

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:45.523
Most models ignore this, assuming sampling is
non-informative.

00:00:45.603 --> 00:00:50.191
Ignoring clinical presence yields sub-optimal,
non-transportable models;

00:00:50.271 --> 00:00:52.460
modelling it explicitly is the fix.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.363
A key challenge is heterogeneity: the same patient
population can appear very differently

00:00:58.443 --> 00:01:00.692
depending on the observation process,

00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:03.966
and that process shifts across countries, over time,

00:01:04.046 --> 00:01:06.421
and even between weekdays and weekends.

00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:09.757
The literature has studied covariate and label shift,

00:01:09.837 --> 00:01:13.661
but shift in the observation process itself is
under-explored.

00:01:13.741 --> 00:01:19.830
DeepJoint asks how explicitly modelling clinical
presence makes survival models more robust to it.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:26.064
The paper contributes a deep joint model that
treats clinical presence as multi-task learning.

00:01:26.144 --> 00:01:29.852
A shared recurrent embedding feeds four heads:
longitudinal,

00:01:29.932 --> 00:01:33.135
inter-observation timing, missingness, and survival,

00:01:33.215 --> 00:01:38.185
all trained together by maximising a combined
likelihood with dynamic weighting.

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:42.478
The result is a representation that encodes the
observation process,

00:01:42.558 --> 00:01:46.960
giving both a predictive edge and robustness when
that process changes.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:54.234
A Long Short Term Memory network extracts an
embedding from each patient's irregular sequence of laboratory tests.

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:59.617
This embedding drives three clinical-presence
heads: a longitudinal head predicting next

00:01:59.697 --> 00:02:02.064
test values under a Gaussian likelihood,

00:02:02.144 --> 00:02:06.835
a missingness head predicting which tests appear
under a Bernoulli likelihood,

00:02:06.915 --> 00:02:10.506
and a timing head modelling the inter-observation
intensity.

00:02:10.586 --> 00:02:14.360
A DeepSurv head models survival under Cox
proportional hazards.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.418
The four losses are combined by dynamic weighting,

00:02:17.498 --> 00:02:21.150
balanced by a hyperparameter alpha, and optimised
end to end.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.363
Experiments use MIMIC-three,

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:29.374
an intensive-care database of anonymised laboratory
tests for over thirty-eight thousand

00:02:29.454 --> 00:02:33.200
patients at Beth Israel Deaconess between 2001 and 2012.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.621
Restricting to those surviving the first
twenty-four hours leaves a cohort of 30,834 patients.

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:46.247
Models predict in-hospital survival from the
embedding at the last observation in that first day,

00:02:46.327 --> 00:02:51.370
compared by time-dependent concordance index and
Brier score at one, seven,

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.600
and fourteen days.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.948
On a random population split,

00:02:55.028 --> 00:03:02.506
the three proposed methods deliver
competitive-to-best discrimination against models using the same inputs.

00:03:02.586 --> 00:03:06.533
Strikingly, DeepJoint, which sees only laboratory values,

00:03:06.613 --> 00:03:11.760
already outperforms both an LSTM that ignores
clinical presence and GRU-D,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.303
which consumes missingness as input.

00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:18.894
So modelling the observation process, even without
feeding it in,

00:03:18.974 --> 00:03:21.366
yields a more predictive embedding.

00:03:21.446 --> 00:03:26.240
Fine-tuning adds a further edge, reaching a one-day
C-index of 0.878.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.712
The approach decomposes into three variants against
six baselines.

00:03:30.792 --> 00:03:35.453
DeepJointFeature, which adds clinical-presence
features to the joint model,

00:03:35.533 --> 00:03:39.751
improves over plain DeepJoint and matches a strong
feature baseline.

00:03:39.831 --> 00:03:45.251
The fine-tuned variant reaches the highest
population-level discrimination but overfits

00:03:45.331 --> 00:03:47.526
when the observation process shifts.

00:03:47.606 --> 00:03:49.612
Across the robustness experiment,

00:03:49.692 --> 00:03:54.922
DeepJointFeature best combines strong
discrimination with proximity to the diagonal,

00:03:55.002 --> 00:03:57.640
transferring most reliably across settings.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:03.735
The headline numbers: a concordance index of 0.878
at the one-day horizon,

00:04:03.815 --> 00:04:07.102
on a cohort of 30,834 MIMIC-three patients.

00:04:07.182 --> 00:04:13.288
DeepJoint jointly captures three dimensions of
clinical presence, longitudinal,

00:04:13.368 --> 00:04:19.161
timing, and missingness, alongside survival, across
horizons of one, seven,

00:04:19.241 --> 00:04:20.570
and fourteen days.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.180
The takeaway: the way clinical data are sampled is
itself informative,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.475
and jointly modelling that observation process with
survival produces predictions that

00:04:30.555 --> 00:04:35.339
are both more accurate and markedly more robust
when clinical practice changes.

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:37.740
Clinical presence is signal, not noise,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:41.250
and modelling it makes medical predictions transportable.
